@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Deploying a self-hosted Fizzy instance
+
+The root Kamal configuration deploys the open-source, SQLite version of Fizzy to a single Linux server. The app, its SQLite databases, and Solid Queue run in the Fizzy container; Kamal Proxy runs alongside it to provide HTTPS.
+
+## Before you start
+
+- A Linux server with Docker and SSH access. Kamal defaults to the `root` SSH user; if you use another Docker-enabled account, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`.
+- A hostname whose DNS A/AAAA record points to the server. Allow inbound ports 80 and 443 for Kamal Proxy and port 22 for SSH.
+- A workstation with Docker, Ruby/Bundler, and the repository's dependencies installed (`bundle install`) to run Kamal.
+
+## Configure the deployment
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+- Replace `203.0.113.10` with the server's public IP.
+- Replace `fizzy.example.com` with the hostname for your instance (it is set once at the top of the file and used for both the proxy and email links).
+- Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to match your mail provider. The example uses authenticated SMTP with STARTTLS on port 587.
+
+Create the local Kamal secrets file and fill it with real values:
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+Generate a stable Rails `SECRET_KEY_BASE` with `bin/rails secret` and paste the output into `.kamal/secrets`. Do not regenerate it between deploys. Set `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` to your mail provider credentials. Generate a VAPID pair for browser push notifications with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"'
+```
+
+Paste the two values into `.kamal/secrets`. Never commit that file. If you add encrypted Rails credentials to your fork, add `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` under `env.secret` in `config/deploy.yml` and set its value in `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+## Deploy
+
+From the repository root, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal builds Fizzy, bootstraps its local image registry and Kamal Proxy, obtains the HTTPS certificate, then starts the app. Subsequent releases use `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+The app has only a `web` role: Puma starts Solid Queue in the same container, so no separate jobs server or database service is needed. `DATABASE_ADAPTER=sqlite` selects the four production SQLite databases. The named Docker volume `fizzy-storage` is mounted at `/rails/storage`, preserving those databases and local uploads when Kamal replaces the app container. This protects data across deploys, but it is not a backup; back up the volume and the server.
diff --git a/test/kamal_deployment_config_test.rb b/test/kamal_deployment_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..826ec93
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/kamal_deployment_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class KamalDeploymentConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  test "self-hosted deployment runs a single SQLite app with persistent storage and proxy SSL" do
+    config = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], config.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal 1, config.all_hosts.size
+    assert_empty config.accessories
+    assert config.registry.local?
+    assert config.proxy.ssl?
+    assert_equal 1, config.proxy.hosts.size
+    assert_equal config.proxy.hosts.first, config.env.clear["APP_HOST"]
+    assert_equal true, config.env.clear["ASSUME_SSL"]
+    assert_equal true, config.env.clear["FORCE_SSL"]
+    assert_equal "sqlite", config.env.clear["DATABASE_ADAPTER"]
+    assert_equal true, config.env.clear["SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA"]
+    assert_includes config.raw_config.volumes, "fizzy-storage:/rails/storage"
+    %w[ SECRET_KEY_BASE SMTP_USERNAME SMTP_PASSWORD VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY ].each do |secret|
+      assert_includes config.env.secret_keys, secret
+    end
+  end
+end
